Z={
1:"Jñāneśvar, like many commentators, opens each chapter with praise of the guru. Many modern teachers of yoga and psychology have taken up the guṇa framework because it is simple to use: three tendencies, always present, always shifting. It describes moods, foods, homes, cities and even times of day.",
2:"The cosmic cycle of creation and dissolution was described in 8.17–19 and 9.7–8. Commentators note that the freed soul is not dragged along by these cycles. Madhusūdana says even the gods are born and dissolved, but the knower is not. The Brahma Sūtras end with the words anāvṛttiḥ śabdāt, “there is no return, as scripture declares” (4.4.22).",
3:"Commentators note that the Lord calls prakṛti “my womb”, mama yoniḥ: nature is not opposed to the Divine but belongs to it. Some readers find here a basis for reverence toward nature and for the image of the Goddess as Mother Nature. Śākta traditions see in prakṛti the Devī herself, the creative power of the Lord.",
4:"Commentators note that the verse calls the Lord pitā, father, of all forms in all wombs. Śrī Vaiṣṇava teachers say this is why every being deserves kindness: all are the Lord’s children. Jain and Buddhist ethics reach a similar respect for all life by different reasoning.",
5:"Commentators note that the Self is called dehī, “the embodied one”, and avyaya, imperishable, in the same line. The binding is real in experience but not in essence. Śaṅkara says the guṇas seem to bind because the Self is mistaken for the body. Rāmānuja says the self is truly bound by karma, but its nature remains pure.",
6:"Commentators note anagha, “sinless one”, used for Arjuna here: a gentle reminder that he is fit for the teaching. Madhusūdana says attachment to knowledge binds when one thinks “I am learned”. Many teachers warn against spiritual pride, the subtle sense of being better than others because of one’s practice or knowledge.",
7:"Commentators note that rajas is called rāgātmaka, “of the nature of passion”. Śaṅkara links it to the restless desire that keeps beings busy. Modern life, with its emphasis on productivity and constant stimulation, is often described as rājasika. The Gītā does not ask us to stop working, but to notice when work is driven by craving.",
8:"Commentators note that tamas is said to delude all embodied beings: no one is entirely free of it. Śaṅkara says it covers knowledge as clouds cover the sun. Some modern readers compare tamas to depression or burnout, though the Gītā’s idea is broader. The remedy offered in Indian traditions is gentle activity, light food and good company.",
9:"Commentators note jñānam āvṛtya, “covering knowledge”: tamas does not destroy wisdom; it hides it. When tamas lifts, knowledge reappears. Śaṅkara says the three guṇas each bind to something different, so the remedy for each differs. The Gītā’s three-part map helps one see which remedy is needed.",
10:"Commentators note that the three guṇas are compared to three strands of one rope: never fully separate. Śaṅkara says one prevails by suppressing the other two. In Āyurveda, the doṣas vāta, pitta and kapha are related but different ideas; practitioners often speak of the guṇas and doṣas together when advising on diet and routine.",
11:"Commentators note sarva-dvāreṣu, “through all the gates”: sattva shows through every sense, not only the mind. Śaṅkara says even the body feels light and clear. Many people recognise this state after a good night’s sleep, a walk in nature, or a period of quiet prayer. The verse invites noticing it and returning to it.",
12:"Commentators note bharatarṣabha, “bull of the Bharatas”: Arjuna, a man of action, knows rajas well. Madhusūdana says rajas is useful for one who must act, as long as it serves sattva. A warrior, a doctor in an emergency, a leader in a crisis all need rajas, guided by clarity.",
13:"Commentators note kuru-nandana, “joy of the Kurus”: Kṛṣṇa addresses Arjuna affectionately even while describing the dullest guṇa. Madhusūdana says tamas is the opposite of sattva in every way: darkness for light, inertia for activity, confusion for knowledge. Recognising it is the first step out of it.",
14:"Commentators note amala, “stainless”, used for the worlds reached in sattva. Śaṅkara says these are the worlds of those who know the highest principles, such as Brahmaloka. The verse encourages cultivating sattva in daily life, so that clarity is the natural state at the end.",
15:"Commentators note pralaya, dissolution, used here for death: each death is a small dissolution, as the end of a kalpa is a great one. Śaṅkara says the guṇa dominant at death shapes the next birth. The teaching is not meant to frighten but to show that life’s habits have long consequences.",
16:"Commentators note sukṛta, good action: its fruit is sāttvika and nirmala, stainless. Madhusūdana says good actions bring clarity, which makes further good actions easier. The guṇas form self-reinforcing cycles: clarity breeds clarity, restlessness breeds restlessness, dullness breeds dullness.",
17:"Commentators note that the three products named here, knowledge, greed and confusion, are inner states that anyone can observe. Śaṅkara says knowing these effects helps one recognise their causes. Some teachers suggest an evening review: what arose in me today, and which guṇa was behind it?",
18:"Commentators note jaghanya-guṇa, “the lowest guṇa”: tamas is ranked below rajas, because rajas at least moves. Madhusūdana says one should rise step by step, not leap from tamas to sattva. Many traditions advise this gradual path: first activity, then clarity, then freedom.",
19:"Commentators note that the seer sees “no doer other than the guṇas”. This does not deny responsibility; it changes its quality. Madhusūdana says this vision makes one free from pride in success and guilt in failure. The Buddhist teaching of non-self (anattā) reaches a similar freedom by a different path.",
20:"Commentators note deha-samudbhavān, “from which the body arises”: the body itself is made of the guṇas. Śaṅkara says going beyond them means no longer identifying with the body. Rāmānuja says it means the self no longer bound by matter. The fruit, amṛta, immortality, recalls 2.15 and 13.12.",
21:"Commentators note that Arjuna calls Kṛṣṇa prabhu, “master”, a sign of reverence. Madhusūdana says the question shows Arjuna now wants freedom for himself, not only to understand it. Teachers note that this is the right way to learn any ideal: ask what it looks like in practice.",
22:"Commentators note that the free person does not even long for sattva when it fades. This is remarkable: most spiritual seekers cling to good states. Ramana Maharshi used to say that the Self is beyond all states, even the most peaceful ones, which come and go.",
23:"Commentators note the word āsīna, “sitting”: the free person rests in himself. Śaṅkara says he stays firm in the Self. Rāmānuja says he is not moved by the play of the guṇas because he knows his true nature. Madhusūdana compares him to a mountain unmoved by winds.",
24:"Commentators note dhīra, steady or wise, and tulya-nindātma-saṃstuti, the same in blame and praise of oneself. Śaṅkara says the free person is unaffected by what others say. Many saints faced both praise and abuse calmly. Mīrābāī, criticised by her royal in-laws for her devotion to Kṛṣṇa, kept singing.",
25:"Commentators note sarvārambha-parityāgī, “giving up all selfish undertakings”, which also appeared in 12.16. Śaṅkara says the free person does only what is needed to sustain the body. Rāmānuja says he does all as service to the Lord. The same phrase links the devotee and the one beyond the guṇas.",
26:"Commentators note sevate, “serves”: devotion here is service as well as love. Madhusūdana says the devotee crosses the guṇas by the Lord’s grace, not by effort alone. The verse ties chapter 14 to chapters 7 (7.14) and 12: by refuge in the Lord, māyā of the guṇas is crossed.",
27:"Commentators note sukha aikāntika, “absolute bliss”: joy that does not depend on anything else. Madhva says the Lord is the source of the bliss of the freed. The chapter ends by pointing beyond the guṇas to their source.",
}
for _k,_t in Z.items():
    _a=DEEP.get(_k,('','',''))
    DEEP[_k]=(_a[0],(_a[1]+' '+_t).strip(),_a[2])
